eage and potential for future alliances."}, {"sourceSlug": "stefan-milutin", "sourceName": "Stefan Milutin", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "brnjača", "targetName": "Brnjača", "context": "Stefan Milutin was Brnjača's younger brother, who later became King of Serbia after her death in 1264, carrying forward the dynastic legacy."}], "places": [{"name": "Ras, Serbia", "role": "Dynastic Center"}, {"name": "Brnjaci, Serbia", "role": "Family Estate"}, {"name": "Serbia, Europe", "role": "Homeland"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Brnjača", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "brnjača"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q23583009", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brnja%C4%8Da"], "tags": ["Religious Figures", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 4, "significanceNarrative": "Brnjača's primary significance lies in her potential as a diplomatic asset for the Nemanjić dynasty, whose early death prevented a potentially impactful royal marriage. Her story illuminates the strategic role of princesses in medieval Balkan statecraft.", "significanceCategory": "local"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-07T19:39:48Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42404599336", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Brnjača was a Serbian princess, the daughter of King Stefan Uroš I and Queen Helen of Anjou. Her brothers were Stefan Dragutin and Stefan Milutin. By birth, she was member of the Nemanjić dynasty, ruling family of the Kingdom of Serbia.", "newValue": "Brnjača, a princess of the Nemanjić dynasty, represented a crucial, albeit unrealized, diplomatic asset for the Kingdom of Serbia during the mid-13th century. Born to King Stefan Uroš I and Queen Helen of Anjou, her life, though cut short by her death in 1264, placed her at the nexus of Serbian royal ambitions and the broader European dynastic landscape. Her existence underscored the strategic importance of royal progeny, particularly daughters, in forging alliances and securing peace through marriage pacts, a common practice among medieval European courts.

